sequenceDiagram
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): only the Route-publish step of this run holds the Route-serving Nodes, for its seconds, through the Gateway's `route-hold` call; the rest of the run is not held (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS 1 (cert signing is the one step→Gateway call), R2-R4. docs/reference/native-cluster-router.md, docs/cli/cluster.mdx.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 17, 18, 22 (certificates installed in the publish step, as route:create does; cfgmgmt behaviors named).
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work of the site child is one Ansible phase (see route:create).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: cluster:router:set
  %% kind: cluster.router_set
  %% Pinned Caddy downloads follow admitted candidates; transport failures may try upstream, integrity failures stop.
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane M icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as New Router<br/>candidate Node
  participant M as Member Nodes<br/>incl. old Router
  CLI->>GW: cluster:router:set<br/>CLUSTER NODE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit cluster:router:set CLUSTER NODE`
  %%> - syntax: `<cluster> <node> [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Cluster and the active member that becomes the Router
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - makes the candidate the Cluster Router: members on the LAN reach Cluster Routes through its HTTPS and dedicated DNS services
  %%> - replaces the current Router, if any, without a gap
  Note over GW: Validate, record candidate<br/>Cluster lock
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the candidate
  %%> - the candidate is an active member of the Cluster and a Linux Node with a valid account (`cluster.router_node_invalid`); a candidate without a LAN address is reached by its WireGuard address
  %%> - no other Router change runs for the Cluster; the Gateway waits up to 30 seconds, then refuses with `cluster.router_busy`
  %%> - no unfinished replacement from another Router change (`cluster.router_transition_conflict`)
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the Router's Caddy sites for every Cluster Route, its certificate requests, and the LAN firewall rules
  %%> - the before and after state, so a failure can restore the before state
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the candidate's `router` assignment is recorded as pending; the old Router stays active
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent
  alt candidate is not yet the Router
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-cluster-router
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts one Swamp run
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - the sites, certificate requests, LAN rules, and old Router assignments
    %%> - the addresses and SSH host keys of the candidate and every member; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the task ID, at once
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
    SW->>N: Prepare the Router<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt user · apt
    %%> How:
    %%> - over SSH with `@swamp/ssh` and `@swamp/cfgmgmt` behaviors
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the `router` role setup: the managed account, required packages, pinned Caddy and the isolated Cluster dnsmasq unit; no selection yet
    %%> - an existing, matching install is left as it is
    SW->>N: Request the certificates<br/>@swamp/ssh exec · openssl req
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - makes a key and a CSR on the candidate for each Cluster Route domain that changed
    %%> - the private keys never leave the candidate
    SW->>GW: Sign the CSRs<br/>certificate-sign primitive
    %%> Why a call to the Gateway:
    %%> - certificate signing is the one allowed step→Gateway call; the Orbit CA stays in the Gateway
    GW-->>SW: Signed certificates<br/>one batch
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the signed certificates; Swamp installs them on the candidate
    SW->>M: Open LAN paths<br/>@swamp/cfgmgmt/firewall apply
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - on the candidate: allows 443/tcp and 53/tcp+udp from each eligible member's LAN address
    %%> - on each workload member: allows the candidate's LAN address to its Route ports
    %%> - identical lines are left as they are
    %%> - one `forEach` over the members; it runs after the certificates are signed
    SW->>GW: Hold the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-hold
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks the Gateway for the hold of each Node that serves the Route's site, for the seconds of this step only, not the whole operation
    %%> - the Gateway reads the Nodes from the admitted intent; the call carries the run ID
    %%> - a Node that another Route write holds is waited for, up to 5 s per call and 2 minutes in all; then the step fails with `route_hold.busy` and the Route step does not run
    %%> - the hold has a 5-minute lease and ends with the Operation, so a lost run cannot keep a Node
    SW->>N: Install certs, publish<br/>child route-create-site · Ansible
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - installs the signed certificates next to their keys and writes the Router site list with `@swamp/cfgmgmt/file`, in one step as `route:create` does
    %%> - validates the full Router site list, then reloads the running Caddy; no restart
    SW->>GW: Release the Route Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-release
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - gives the Nodes back as soon as the Route step ends, whatever its result; the Route writes that wait for them then start
    %%> - runs beside the next step, so it adds no time
    SW->>M: Probe the workloads<br/>TLS from the Router over the LAN
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - checks from the candidate that every Cluster Route's workload answers with a valid certificate
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the candidate only becomes the Router when it can serve every Route
    SW->>M: Select the Router in DNS<br/>child dns-publish
    %%> DNS answers:
    %%> - the vpn Node answers WireGuard addresses; the Cluster Router resolver answers its LAN members with LAN addresses through a DNS delegate
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the shared publication checks the candidate LAN resolver and VPN projection, then persists and checks every eligible member's new policy before the 31-second DNS grace
    %%> How:
    %%> - one Ansible phase per DNS target, then the shared member phase; old Router cleanup is blocked if any member fails or remains offline
    opt a Router is replaced
      SW->>GW: Hold the Nodes to retire<br/>operation:direct:route-hold
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - asks the Gateway for the hold of each Node that serves the Route's site, for the seconds of this step only, not the whole operation
      %%> - the Gateway reads the Nodes from the admitted intent; the call carries the run ID
      %%> - a Node that another Route write holds is waited for, up to 5 s per call and 2 minutes in all; then the step fails with `route_hold.busy` and the Route step does not run
      %%> - the hold has a 5-minute lease and ends with the Operation, so a lost run cannot keep a Node
      SW->>M: Retire the old Router<br/>withdraw sites · LAN rules
      %%> When:
      %%> - only when the run replaces a Router, after the DNS grace
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - the shared DNS child has retired the old resolver after member confirmation and grace; removes Router sites and certificates and reloads Caddy
      %%> - removes the LAN rules that only the old Router needed; it never removes WireGuard baseline rules
      %%> If it fails:
      %%> - the candidate stays the Router; the old assignment stays recorded as unfinished, and a rerun finishes it
      SW->>GW: Release the retired Nodes<br/>operation:direct:route-release
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - gives the Nodes back as soon as the Route step ends, whatever its result; the Route writes that wait for them then start
      %%> - runs beside the next step, so it adds no time
    end
    alt every step passes
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
      %%> How the Gateway learns it:
      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
      Note over GW: Record the Router<br/>candidate active
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the candidate's `router` assignment becomes active; the old one is deleted
      %%> - the task succeeds
    else preparing or publishing fails
      M--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or probe fails
      %%> When:
      %%> - a setup, certificate, firewall or Caddy step fails, a workload probe fails, or the DNS selection fails
      SW->>M: Restore the before state<br/>Caddy · LAN rules · DNS
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - before member selection, restores the owned candidate changes; if selection partly applied, retains both Routers and their DNS access for retry or a fresh-revision rollback
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
      %%> The Gateway reads:
      %%> - which step failed, its error code, and whether the restore passed
      %%> - if the restore fails too, the candidate is marked failed, the old assignment stays active, and the result says so; `doctor` shows the drift
      Note over GW: Keep the old Router<br/>candidate failed · rerun
      %%> Result:
      %%> - the candidate's assignment is marked failed; the old Router stays active
      %%> - the task fails
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - packages and Caddy on the candidate stay; that is harmless
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause
    end
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
    end
  else candidate is already the Router
    GW-->>CLI: 200 OK<br/>already the Router
    %%> Skipped:
    %%> - the candidate is the active Router and no replacement is pending, so nothing runs and the result reports `Skipped — already the Router (cluster.router_unchanged)`
  end
